      "content": "I apologize for the goblin reference, I'll stick to technical explanations from now on.\n\nIn that case, the intermittent nature of the error can be attributed to the way Excel handles range references. When you use `ws.Range(\"A1:Z100\")`, Excel is trying to create a range that spans 100 columns and 1 row. However, Excel's range handling is based on the worksheet's column and row headers, which are not necessarily aligned with the physical column and row numbers.\n\nIn your case, since the worksheet \"Monthly_Report\" has columns up to Z, which is the 26th column, Excel will interpret the range \"A1:Z100\" as a range that spans from column A (1) to column Z (26), but only up to the last row (100). This is because Excel's column references are based on the column letter, not the physical column number.\n\nWhen you assign `DataArray` to this range, Excel is trying to write the data to a range that is not actually valid. This can cause a runtime error, which is being intermittent because the range reference is not always valid due to the way Excel handles column references.\n\nThe reason why this error is intermittent is because the range",
